    ":question/math-academy-id": "e-983",
    ":question/problem": "Use the table below to plot the graph of $y=3x-2$.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |",
    ":question/worked-solution": "First, we fill the table. Given a value of $x$, we can find the corresponding $y$ by substituting $x$ into the formula $y=3x-2$.\nTherefore,\n\n- if $x=0$ then $y=3(0)-2=-2$,\n- if $x=1$ then $y=3(1)-2=1$,\n- if $x=2$ then $y=3(2)-2=4$, and\n- if $x=3$ then $y=3(3)-2=7$.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y$ | $-2$ | $1$ | $4$ | $7$ |\n\nWe plot these $(x,y)$ pairs on the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/09/09e670353b2114b229769e2bd6b3eb4a9895389b818fc3f4ace6997a3e7efa3f.png)\n\nFinally, connecting the points gives us a straight line. This is the graph of $y=3x-2$.\n\n![](images/d2/d2d8891102ce99020f544bd8d82a5ff0454080474ce4267ef5285db92f5b2ca0.png)"
  },
